],["he","יְהוָ֑ה",null,"H3068","HNp"],["he","וַיַּֽאֲמִ֨ינוּ֙",null,"H539","HC/Vhw3mp","c"],["he","בַּֽיהוָ֔ה",null,"H3068","HR/Np","b"],["he","וּבְמֹשֶׁ֖ה",null,"H4872","HC/R/Np","c/b"],["he","עַבְדּֽוֹ",null,"H5650","HNcmsc/Sp3ms"]]},"gl":{"H1696":"speak","H3068":"LORD","H413":"to(wards)","H4872":"Moses","H559":"to say","H1121":"child","H3478":"Israel","H7725":"return","H2583":"to camp","H6440":"before","H6367":"Pi-hahiroth","H996":"between","H4024":"tower","H3220":"sea","H1189":"Baal-zephon","H5226":"before","H5921":"upon","H6547":"Pharaoh","H943":"to perplex","H1992":"they(masc.)","H776":"country/planet","H5462":"to shut","H4057":"mouth","H2388":"strengthen","H853":"[Obj.]","H3820":"heart","H7291":"to pursue","H310":"after","H3513":"honour","H3605":"all","H2428":"Helech","H3045":"to know","H4713":"Egyptian","H3588":"for","H589":"I","H6213":"do","H3651":"right","H5046":"to tell","H4428":"king","H4714":"Egypt","H1272":"to flee","H5971":"people","H2015":"to overturn","H3824":"heart","H5650":"servant/slave","H4100":"what?","H2063":"this","H7971":"depart","H5647":"to serve","H631":"to bind","H7393":"chariot","H3947":"take","H5973":"with","H8337":"six","H3967":"hundred","H977":"to choose","H7991":"third","H3318":"come","H3027":"hand","H7311":"to exalt","H5381":"to overtake","H5483":"swallow","H6571":"horse","H7126":"come","H5375":"raise","H5869":"eye","H2009":"behold","H5265":"to set out","H3372":"to fear","H3966":"much","H6817":"to cry","H1097":"without","H369":"nothing","H6913":"grave","H4191":"to die","H3808":"not","H2088":"this","H1697":"word","H834":"which","H2308":"to cease","H4480":"from","H2896":"pleasant","H408":"not","H3320":"to stand","H7200":"see","H3444":"salvation","H3117":"day","H3254":"again","H5750":"still","H5704":"till","H5769":"enduring","H3898":"to fight","H859":"you(m.s.)","H2790":"to plow/plot","H4294":"rod","H5186":"to stretch","H1234":"to break up/open","H935":"come","H8432":"midst","H3004":"dry land","H2005":"look!","H4397":"messenger","H430":"God","H1980":"went","H4264":"camp","H3212":"to walk","H5982":"pillar","H6051":"cloud","H5975":"stand","H1961":"to be","H2822":"darkness","H215":"to light","H3915":"night","H7307":"spirit","H6921":"east","H5794":"strong","H7760":"make","H2724":"dry ground","H4325":"water","H2346":"wall","H3225":"right","H8040":"left","H821":"watch","H1242":"morning","H8259":"to look","H784":"fire","H2000":"to confuse","H5493":"remove","H212":"wheel","H4818":"chariot","H5090":"to lead","H3517":"heaviness","H5127":"to flee","H6437":"to turn","H386":"strong","H7125":"befall","H5287":"to shake","H3680":"to cover","H7604":"to remain","H259":"one","H3467":"to save","H1931":"he/she/it","H8193":"lips","H1419":"large","H539":"be faithful"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Beelsephon, means “the lord of the watch-tower.” Some think an idol was thus denominated, whose office it was to prevent people from quitting the country. How vain were his efforts against God’s people!"],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","In. Between craggy mountains and the Red Sea. (Haydock)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","And he will. Protestants falsely translate, “that he may,” &c., contrary to the Hebrew and other versions. (Worthington)"],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","People, fit for war, who could be got ready on such short warning. Ezechiel (apud[in the writings of] Eusebius) makes the number amount to a million."],["haydock",7,null,"jerome","Captains. Septuagint, “Tristatas.” Three men rode on every chariot; which was armed with scythes, to cut down all that came within contact, the chief warrior, with his armour-bearer and charioteer. (St. Gregory of Nyssa) (Haydock) — Or these three captains may very probably be the three chief officers of state, (Calmet) or the generals of cavalry, and of infantry, and the chief treasurer, or receiver of taxes, principes equitum peditumque erant, & tributorum. (St. Jerome)"],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Hand. Without any dread, Numbers xv. 30. (Calmet) — All the army of Egypt could do nothing against them. Yet presently, at their approach, the Hebrews were suffered to fall into dismay, that they might learn not to confide in their multitudes, and might pray with greater earnestness for protection, ver. 10."],["haydock",12,null,"haydock","Wilderness. This is the language of dastardly souls. They had begun to be almost in love with their chains. Every difficulty gives them occasion to repine at the gracious purposes of God, and the exertions of his servant Moses. But God bears patiently with the defects of a carnal and long oppressed nation, ver. 13. (Haydock)— The wiser sort pray to God, while others thus upbraid Moses."],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Ever. They saw their floating carcasses the following morning. Hebrew, “you shall not see the Egyptians any more as you see them at present.” They were not in the same condition."],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Peace. You will not have to draw a sword. The Syriac subjoins, “Therefore Moses cried unto the Lord,” which connects this with the following verse. (Calmet)"],["haydock",15,null,"bernard","Criest. — “A vehement desire is a cry, which reaches the ears of the Lord.” (St. Bernard)"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","To pursue. God did not restrain the perverse will of the Egyptians; but suffered them to be guided by their blind passions, and to rush presumptuously into the bed of the sea. If the retiring of its waters had been owing to any natural cause, this wise nation could not be ignorant but that, at the stated time, the ebbing would cease, and consequently that they would be overtaken by the waters. But the waters stood up like walls on both sides, and they were so infatuated as to suppose that the miracle would be continued for their protection. (Haydock)"],["haydock",20,null,"haydock","A dark cloud, and enlightening the night. It was a dark cloud to the Egyptians; but enlightened the night to the Israelites, by giving them a great light."],["haydock",21,null,"haydock","Wind. This served to dry up the sandy channel of the Red Sea, which was mixed with mud and weeds. It blew from the east, Kodim, or from Arabia. — Divided, some say into 12 parts or divisions, Psalm cxxxv. 13. But the words of the psalmist may be verified by the sea opening a spacious passage, such as was requisite for so many millions to travel through, (Haydock) e.g. a distance of perhaps 18 miles, in so short a space of time. Silara Adrichomius thinks the breadth of the division would not be less than nine miles."],["haydock",24,null,"haydock","Watch. About four o’clock. The Hebrews divided the night into three equal parts, (Calmet) or four, consisting each of three hours, (Menochius) which varied in length as the night was longer. (Haydock) — Slew many by his thunderbolts, as Artapanus relates, and the Scripture elsewhere insinuates. (Chap. xv. 6, 12; Psalm lxxvi. 16, 18; Josephus, [Antiquities?] ii. 7."],["haydock",25,null,"haydock","Lord. thus they reluctantly confess his might, and are forced to glory Him in their destruction. Their change is only the effect of fear and temporal danger, ver. 18. (Haydock)"],["haydock",31,null,"haydock","Sea-shore. The Hebrews would thus again be enriched by their spoils. (Calmet) — Servant. Those who believe God, submit to the directions of his ambassadors. (St. Jerome in Philemon 5.) In this merited catastrophe of the Egyptians, which fixed the last seal to the mission of Moses, the fathers contemplate how God’s servants are rescued by baptism, and by the merits of Jesus Christ, from Satan and from all sin. (1 Corinthians x. 1, 4; Origen, hom. 5.) (Haydock)"],["challoner",20,null,"challoner","A dark cloud, and enlightening the night.... It was a dark cloud to the Egyptians; but enlightened the night to the Israelites by giving them a great light."]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"],"jerome":["Jerome",1,1,420,"https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/#jerome"],"bernard":["Bernard of Clairvaux",1,0,1153,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/02498d.htm"],"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"]},"fa":[[14,null,0,"13 Ascetic Discourses","Discourse 9 -- Second Discourse on Poverty",0,523,"But fear thou not, neither be thou afraid, for instead of Moses, Jesus is with thee, for like as Moses clave to the congregation, even so also doth Christ cleave to thy soul, and He saith unto thy tortured and afflicted mind that which was said by Moses to the Jews, \"The Lord shall fight for you, and ye shall hold your peace. Therefore thou shalt not be in fear as were the people, but thou shalt rouse up, and watch like Moses, and cry out to the Lord even as he cried out. …"],[15,null,0,"13 Ascetic Discourses","Discourse 9 -- Second Discourse on Poverty",0,523,"Moses prayed the whole night with much crying out and suffering, and at the morning watch the Lord said to him, \"Why criest thou before Me? Incline thy hand over the sea, and divide it, and the Hebrews shall pass over it, but the Egyptians shall be drowned therein. Now all the things which happened at that time are a type of those which shall be done unto thee. …"],[19,24,1,"The Proof of the Gospel","Book 5",1,339,"… \"And the angel of the Lord that went before the children of Israel, removed and went behind them; and the pillar of the cloud also removed from before them.\" And as in the former passage the Lord is introduced as answering the men of the old time in human form, so also is He here by the cloud. For it is said afterwards: \"And it came to pass in the morning-watch, that the Lord looked upon the camp of the Egyptians in a pillar of fire and cloud. And God answered Moses in the pillar of the cloud through the whole of the wanderings in the wilderness.\" …"],[21,null,2,"Homilies on John","Homily 43",2,407,"The Jews under the guidance of Moses passed over the Red Sea, but that case is widely different from this. He did all with prayer and as a servant, but Christ with absolute power. There when the south wind blew, the water yielded so as to make them pass over on dry land, but here the miracle was greater. For the sea retaining its proper nature so bare its Lord upon its surface, thus testifying to the Scripture which saith, \"Who walketh upon the sea as upon a pavement.\""],[22,null,3,"Expositions on the Psalms","Psalm 107",3,430,"This people of God, freed from a great and broad Egypt, is led, as through the Red Sea, that in baptism it may make an end of its enemies. For by the sacrament as it were of the Red Sea, that is by baptism consecrated with the blood of Christ, the pursuing Egyptians, the sins, are washed away."],[22,null,4,"On the Baptism of Christ","ON THE BAPTISM OF CHRIST",4,395,"Again, according to the view of the inspired Paul, the people itself, by passing through the Red Sea, proclaimed the good tidings of salvation by water. The people passed over, and the Egyptian king with his host was engulfed, and by these actions this Sacrament was foretold. For even now, whensoever the people is in the water of regeneration, fleeing from Egypt, from the burden of sin, it is set free and saved; but the devil with his own servants (I mean, of course, the spirits of evil), is choked with grief, and perishes, deeming the salvation of men to be his own misfortune."],[22,null,5,"Commentary on Revelation",null,5,892,"And since we cannot recount everything that was done in Egypt through Moses, let us set down a few of the things that were done by them after the departure of the people of Israel from Egypt. The sons of Israel, in order to escape Pharaoh and the Egyptians, passed through the midst of the sea on dry ground, and the waters were for them like a wall on the right and on the left (Exod. 14:22). Likewise, the people of the nations, in order to escape the Devil and all his followers, passed through the water of baptism. …"],[31,null,6,"On the Spirit","ON THE SPIRIT 14.33",6,379,"… \"The people,\" it is written, \"believed the Lord and his servant Moses. Moses then is joined with God, not with the Spirit; and he was a type not of the Spirit, but of Christ. For at that time in the ministry of the law, he by means of himself typified \"the Mediator between God and men. Moses, when mediating for the people in things pertaining to God, was not a minister of the Spirit; for the law was given, \"ordained by angels in the hand of a mediator,\" namely Moses, in accordance with the summons of the people, \"Speak thou with us, ...but let not God speak with us. …"]],"fau":[["philoxenus-of-mabbug","Philoxenus of Mabbug",0,"oriental",null,0],["eusebius","Eusebius of Caesarea",0,"ecclesiastical-writer",null,1,339],["chrysostom","John Chrysostom",1,"father",null,1,407],["augustine","Augustine of Hippo",1,"father",null,1,430],["gregory-nyssa","Gregory of Nyssa",0,"father",null,1,395],["berengaudus","Berengaudus",0,"medieval",null,0],["basil","Basil the Great",1,"father",null,1,379]],"ftr":[["E. A. Wallis Budge, The Discourses of Philoxenus (1894)"],["W. J. Ferrar, The Proof of the Gospel (SPCK, 1920)"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 1, vol. 14 (1889)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf114"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 1, vol. 8 (1888)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf108"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 5 (1893)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf205"],["John Litteral, released into the public domain"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 8 (1895)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf208"]]}